WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 111: George Washington and the Newburgh Conspiracy: The Spectacles That Saved the Republic

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:21.280
[Rebecca] Today, we visit the snow dusted cantonment of the Continental Army at Newburgh, New York, in the tense, volatile spring of 1783, to witness the moment American civilian democracy came closest to dying before it was even born.

4
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:31.520
[Rebecca] With the Revolutionary War I following the surrender at Yorktown, the Continental Army had gone years without pay, their families facing bankruptcy and destitution.

5
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:43.440
[Rebecca] Tempted by mutinous anonymous circulars, and backed by nationalist politicians in Philadelphia, disgruntled officers prepared to march an armed military force onto Congress and crown a dictator.

6
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.360
[Rebecca] Standing alone between the young republic and a military dictatorship was General George Washington.

7
00:00:49.360 --> 00:01:00.640
[Rebecca] In a dramatic confrontation inside a wooden meeting hall known as the Temple of Virtue, Washington saved American democracy, not with an army, but with a pair of reading spectacles.

8
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.920
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full moving story.

9
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:29.520
[George] The decisive military campaign of the Revolutionary War had concluded 16 months earlier with the glorious surrender of Lord Cornwallys at Yorktown in October 1781.

10
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:42.000
[George] British troops remained garrisoned in New York City, awaiting the conclusion of final peace treaties in Paris, while 10,000 battle-hardened American veterans waited in their wooden huts for the formal end of the war.

11
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.080
[George] Yet inside the Newburg cantonment, an atmosphere of poisonous bitter resentment was festering.

12
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:56.480
[George] The Confederation Congress, operating under the fragile articles of Confederation, possessed no constitutional authority to levy taxes.

13
00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:05.080
[George] Bankrupt, disorganized, and paralyzed by regional squabbles, Congress had failed to pay the soldiers their promised wages for years.

14
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:16.440
[George] Officers had spent their personal fortunes purchasing food, boots and uniforms for their men, mortgaging their family farms and accumulating crushing debts to keep the army in the field.

15
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:26.440
[George] Even worse, Congress showed every sign of repudiating its solemn 1880 promise to provide officers with a half-pay lifetime pension upon the war's conclusion.

16
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:42.360
[George] As peace approached, officers realized with mounting fury that once the formal treaty was signed and the army disbanded, Congress would send them home penniless into destitution, casting aside the veterans who had bled for eight long years to secure American independence.

17
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:53.800
[George] Three hundred miles to the south, in Philadelphia, a powerful faction of nationalist politicians in the Continental Congress watched the Army's mounting anger with cynical political calculation.

18
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:13.800
[George] Led by Superintendent of Finance Robert Morris, his assistant Gouverneur Morris, and young New York Congressman Alexander Hamilton, the nationalists were desperate to establish a powerful, centralized federal government equipped with an independent national taxing power, the 5% impost on imported goods.

19
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:23.240
[George] However, state legislatures in Rhode Island and Virginia obstinately refused to ratify the national tariff, fiercely guarding their local sovereignty.

20
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:31.480
[George] Frustrated by this state obstructionism, the nationalist faction in Philadelphia conceived a dangerous, Machiavellian strategy.

21
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:40.600
[George] They resolved to use the looming threat of the unpaid Continental Army as political leverage to terrify the states into granting Congress taxing authority.

22
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:50.640
[George] In early 1783, an official delegation of officers led by General Alexander McDougall arrived in Philadelphia to deliver an urgent petition to Congress.

23
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.040
[George] We have borne all that men can bear, our property is expended, our private resources are at an end.

24
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.120
[George] Any further experiments on our patience may have fatal effects.

25
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:12.160
[George] Behind closed doors, Gouverneur Morris and Hamilton privately encouraged the officers to keep their muskets loaded and hint at an armed refusal to disband until their financial demands were met.

26
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:22.000
[George] On Monday morning, March 10, 1783, an incendiary political handbill was found circulating secretly through the officers' huts at Newburg.

27
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:37.680
[George] Penned with fiery eloquence by 30-year-old Major John Armstrong J.R., an ambitious aide-de-camp to General Horatio Gates, the anonymous circular urged the officers to cast aside humble petitions and prepare for armed coercion against Congress.

28
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.680
[George] Armstrong's text was raw, passionate, and treasonous.

29
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:51.200
[George] He demanded, can you then consent to be the only sufferers by this revolution, and retiring from the field, grow old in poverty, wretchedness and contempt?

30
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.720
[George] Can you consent to wade through the vile mire of dependency?

31
00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.560
[George] And owe the miserable remnant of that life to charity, which has hitherto been spent to honor.

32
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.480
[George] He called for an unauthorized mass assembly of officers the following morning at the Temple of Virtue.

33
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.680
[George] An enormous wooden hall built by the troops for Masonic meetings and divine services.

34
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.160
[George] The circular outlined a chilling ultimatum if peace arrived.

35
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:24.240
[George] The army should refuse to lay down their arms and instead marched directly onto Philadelphia to compel Congress to pay them at bayonet point.

36
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:37.240
[George] If the war continued, the army should immediately abandon Congress and the civilian population, retreating into the western wilderness to establish their own military empire, leaving the states to face the British alone.

37
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.320
[George] It was nothing less than the blueprint for a military coup d'etat.

38
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:50.440
[George] When a copy of the mutinous circular was brought to General George Washington at his headquarters in the Hasbrock House, the commander-in-chief recoiled in horror.

39
00:05:50.440 --> 00:06:08.360
[George] Washington recognized instantly that an armed military uprising would completely destroy everything the American Revolution had fought to achieve, replacing British monarchical tyranny with the far more brutal spectacle of a military dictatorship along the lines of Oliver Cromwell or Julius Caesar.

40
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:16.280
[George] Yet Washington knew that an angry, armed veteran army could not simply be suppressed by executive fiat or threats of court-martial.

41
00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:23.320
[George] Any heavy-handed attempt to ban the officers from meeting would merely inflame passions and alienate him from the men he loved.

42
00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:29.720
[George] Instead, Washington executed a brilliant, subtle tactical maneuver of psychological warfare.

43
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:37.800
[George] In general orders issued on March 11, Washington formally reprimanded the anonymous circular as irregular and disorderly.

44
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:49.720
[George] But in the very same breath, he cancelled the Tuesday meeting and rescheduled it for Saturday, March 15, ordering that the senior officer present preside over a full discussion of the grievances.

45
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:59.640
[George] Washington pointedly instructed General Horatio Gates, the vain, scheming rival who secretly sympathized with the conspiracy, to chair the gathering.

46
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:10.040
[George] Most crucially, Washington gave every indication that he would not attend the meeting himself, leading the conspirators to assume they would have a free hand to organize their rebellion.

47
00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:19.840
[George] On Saturday noon, March 15, 1783, over five hundred Continental Army officers packed into the drafty wooden hall of the Temple of Virtue.

48
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.080
[George] The atmosphere was charged with defiant, mutinous tension.

49
00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:38.240
[George] General Horatio Gates took his seat at the front podium as chairman, while young conspirators like Armstrong leaned against the log walls, confident that the assembly was prepared to vote overwhelmingly for an armed confrontation with Congress.

50
00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.400
[George] Suddenly, the heavy oak doors at the back of the hall swung open.

51
00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.200
[George] A collective gasp echoed through the room.

52
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:52.960
[George] Striding down the center aisle, dressed in his immaculate blue and buff continental uniform, was General George Washington.

53
00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.640
[George] The room fell into an uncomfortable, icy silence.

54
00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:03.840
[George] The officers, who revered their commander but felt betrayed by civilian politicians, shifted nervously in their benches.

55
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.480
[George] Washington walked straight to the podium.

56
00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.680
[George] Gates, bewildered and visibly intimidated, stepped aside without a word.

57
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:22.720
[George] Washington looked out across the sea of familiar faces, men who had frozen beside him at Valley Forge, charged through the snow at Trenton, and endured the bloody trenches of Yorktown.

58
00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:30.560
[George] Pulling a nine-page handwritten manuscript from his coat pocket, Washington began to read with solemn, measured gravity.

59
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.320
[George] For fifteen minutes, Washington read his prepared address.

60
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:45.840
[George] He denounced the anonymous circular as unpatriotic and treasonous, asking how an American officer could contemplate opening the floodgates of civil war and turning their swords against their own countrymen.

61
00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:53.360
[George] He pleaded with them to have patience, pledging on his sacred honor that he would not rest until Congress satisfied their just claims.

62
00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.800
[George] Yet as Washington read, he could feel that his polished arguments were falling on deaf ears.

63
00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.680
[George] The officers remained cold, sullen, and unyielding.

64
00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:07.880
[George] The deep bitterness of years of neglect was too profound to be soothed by formal rhetoric.

65
00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:13.800
[George] Concluding his speech, Washington paused, realizing that his words had failed to turn the tide.

66
00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:23.640
[George] He reached into his pocket and produced a personal letter from Virginia Congressman Joseph Jones, which explained Congress's financial difficulties and promised relief.

67
00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.720
[George] Washington opened the letter and attempted to read the small handwriting.

68
00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.800
[George] But his eyes were failing him, and his voice began to falter.

69
00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:36.120
[George] He squinted at the page in silence, struggling to decipher the ink.

70
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.520
[George] The hall fell dead silent as the mighty general stood helpless before them.

71
00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:53.480
[George] Reaching slowly into his vest pocket, Washington pulled out something none of his officers had ever seen him wear in public a pair of wireimmed reading spectacles, as he carefully slipped the glasses over his ears.

72
00:09:53.480 --> 00:10:00.520
[George] Washington looked up at his hardened officers and offered a quiet, deeply vulnerable apology that shattered the silence.

73
00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:09.160
[George] Gentlemen, you will permit me to put on my spectacles, for I have not only grown gray, but almost blind in the service of my country.

74
00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.360
[George] The effect was instantaneous and overwhelming.

75
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:18.120
[George] Those simple, unscripted words pierced the armor of the most hardened combat veterans in the room.

76
00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:27.800
[George] The officers suddenly realized the immense, crushing sacrifice their commander had borne for eight years without a single dollar of salary or a single day of leave.

77
00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:33.320
[George] Men who had charged bayonets without flinching broke down and wept openly into their coats.

78
00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:37.960
[George] Shamed by their own rebellion, the officers hung their heads in tears.

79
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:48.000
[George] Without another word, General Washington folded his spectacles, placed the letter back into his coat, and walked quietly out of the Temple of Virtue into the Newburgh snow.

80
00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.600
[George] The moment the door closed behind him, the conspiracy evaporated like morning mist.

81
00:10:53.600 --> 00:11:04.880
[George] Major General Henry Knox immediately leaped to his feet and introduced a resolution affirming the Army's absolute, unshakable loyalty to the United States and the Continental Congress.

82
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:08.960
[George] Rejecting the anonymous circulars with abhorrence and disdain.

83
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.320
[George] General Gates was forced to put the resolution to a vote.

84
00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.920
[George] It passed unanimously without a single dissenting voice.

85
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:24.960
[George] The Newburgh conspiracy was dead, disarmed not by military force, but by the profound humility and vulnerability of one great man.

86
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:35.840
[George] Nine months later, on December 23, 1783, inside the Maryland State House in Annapolis, George Washington performed the crowning act of his life.

87
00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:46.080
[George] Standing before the Continental Congress, he formally surrendered his military commission as Commander-in-Chief and retired to his beloved Mount Vernon as a private citizen.

88
00:11:46.080 --> 00:12:00.880
[George] When King George III of Great Britain was informed that Washington had voluntarily surrendered supreme military power rather than seizing the crown, the British monarch remarked in awe, if he does that, he will be the greatest man in the world.

89
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:14.640
[George] By refusing to become an American Caesar at Newburgh, Washington established the sacred, bedrock constitutional principle of the United States that the military must forever remain subordinate to the elected civilian government.

90
00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.960
[George] It was the moment the American Republic was truly saved.

91
00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:30.280
[Rebecca] George Washington's surrender of his military commission, months later, cemented the supreme principle of civilian control of the military in American constitutional life.

92
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.400
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

93
00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:42.200
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we step into a colonial Boston courtroom with young John Adams to witness his courageous defense of British redcoats in the Boston Massacre.

